00:02:35.824 --> 00:02:37.720
And without further ado, let's begin.

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.241
Hello, and thank you for joining me.

00:02:40.778 --> 00:02:45.157
My name is Osdeibi Acurero, and this talk
is about a problem that many WordPress

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:50.881
team know well: accessibility bug that
remain in a project even when the team

00:02:50.883 --> 00:02:52.807
follow a modern development process.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:57.696
These bugs do not always begin
with a careless coding decision.

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:03.058
Often, they begin much earlier in
the tools we select, the default we

00:03:03.058 --> 00:03:07.654
accept, and the assumption we pass from
one state to the project to the next.

00:03:08.291 --> 00:03:12.694
To understand how that happen,
we need to begin in two places

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:15.269
where most WordPress work start.

00:03:15.982 --> 00:03:17.822
The first one is a clean installation.

00:03:18.095 --> 00:03:19.650
The dashboard is empty.

00:03:19.913 --> 00:03:24.217
There are no pages, no custom block,
no product, and no client content.

00:03:24.703 --> 00:03:28.721
It feel as if the project have
no accessibility problems because

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:30.607
we have not built anything yet.

00:03:31.379 --> 00:03:33.438
The second is the exact opposite.

00:03:34.063 --> 00:03:38.716
A client give us an existing si- site and
say, "Hey, we only need a few changes."

00:03:39.057 --> 00:03:43.945
The site have a theme selected years ago,
plugins installed by different teams,

00:03:44.246 --> 00:03:50.349
custom CSS in several location, a page
builder, code snippet, third-party widget,

00:03:50.473 --> 00:03:52.529
and content copied from older pages.

00:03:53.313 --> 00:03:55.904
In one project, the decision
are about to be made.

00:03:56.039 --> 00:03:59.769
In the other one, the decision are
already embedded in the system.

00:04:00.894 --> 00:04:04.940
Accessibility bugs survive in both
situation for the same reason.

00:04:05.589 --> 00:04:09.956
The user experience is one complete
product, while the development

00:04:09.957 --> 00:04:12.021
process sees separate deliveries.

00:04:12.222 --> 00:04:15.435
For example, separate
component and separate owners.

00:04:15.743 --> 00:04:19.966
This is not just a lecture about
memorizing all the criteria.

00:04:20.111 --> 00:04:23.110
It's not a catalog of keyboard shortcut.

00:04:23.387 --> 00:04:26.531
It's a technical investigation
of the real process failure.

00:04:27.291 --> 00:04:32.169
We will follow one life cycle where
the accessibility bug is born, uh,

00:04:32.229 --> 00:04:36.510
and how WordPress multiply it, how
a modern workflow allow it to reach

00:04:36.519 --> 00:04:41.296
production, why it return after being
fixed, and how to change the workflow

00:04:41.305 --> 00:04:45.465
so the same class of bug become
harder to create and harder to ship.

00:04:46.214 --> 00:04:51.368
By the end of this talk, you will have a
practical way to identify where this bug

00:04:51.476 --> 00:04:58.180
enter a WordPress project by the workflow
phase to stop them and how-- what need to

00:04:58.182 --> 00:05:00.759
change so they are less likely to return.

00:05:01.865 --> 00:05:04.066
The structure is intentionally simple.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.865
First, we will find where
accessibility bugs are born.

00:05:08.368 --> 00:05:14.313
Theme selection, plugin procurement, block
design, configuration, and the assumption

00:05:14.405 --> 00:05:16.810
made before custom development begins.

00:05:17.484 --> 00:05:20.644
Second, we will find why
WordPress multiplied.

00:05:21.298 --> 00:05:25.848
A defect in a reusable block,
template part, global style, or plugin

00:05:25.849 --> 00:05:28.146
integration does not remain local.

00:05:28.693 --> 00:05:31.303
It can be a normal
behavior across the site.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:35.832
Third, we will follow a released
defect through a modern workflow.

00:05:36.354 --> 00:05:39.705
We will focus on the moment
where its meaning is reduced.

00:05:40.331 --> 00:05:44.768
The requirement describes appearance,
the design represents isolated screens,

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:50.615
QA receives incomplete acceptance
criteria, and release have no clear

00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:52.742
authority model for the remaining risk.

00:05:53.544 --> 00:05:56.593
Four: we will find the same bug return.

00:05:57.145 --> 00:06:00.900
A visible page is repaired, but
the theme, plugins, pattern,

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:05.410
configuration, or process that
generated defect remains active.

00:06:06.125 --> 00:06:10.347
Then an ordinary update or
content changes reproduces it.

00:06:10.961 --> 00:06:15.616
Finally, we will build a workflow
that addresses those causes directly.

00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:22.179
Behavioral requirement, safer
reusable defaults, explicit ownership,

00:06:22.553 --> 00:06:27.339
deliberate release decisions, and
protection when WordPress changes.

00:06:28.133 --> 00:06:35.356
The central idea is this: an accessibility
bug is rarely only live in a line of code.

00:06:36.058 --> 00:06:41.037
More often, it's a chain of individual
reasonable decisions that never

00:06:41.037 --> 00:06:44.955
perceive the complete behavior
from the beginning of the project

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:47.167
to production and maintenance.

00:06:47.744 --> 00:06:52.241
On a new WordPress project, the first
accessibility decision often appears

00:06:52.257 --> 00:06:54.541
to be unrelated to accessibility.

00:06:55.292 --> 00:06:59.748
The team chooses a theme because
it matches the brand and includes

00:06:59.763 --> 00:07:01.690
the layout for the client wants.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.094
It selects a form plugin because
it's integrated with the CRM.

00:07:06.594 --> 00:07:10.896
It chooses a block library because
it allows the content team to

00:07:10.897 --> 00:07:13.063
build the landing pages quickly.

00:07:13.535 --> 00:07:18.240
It imports a starter site because
it shortens delivery time.

00:07:18.748 --> 00:07:24.886
It enables a set of global style
because they make visual editing easier.

00:07:25.425 --> 00:07:27.792
So each decision is legitimate.

00:07:28.287 --> 00:07:32.501
The problem is that each decision
also defines the behavior.

00:07:33.278 --> 00:07:39.025
The theme may own the primary navigation,
search, template, pagination, focus

00:07:39.046 --> 00:07:41.765
style, and responsive transitions.

00:07:42.452 --> 00:07:46.937
The form plugin may own
labels, validation, error

00:07:46.937 --> 00:07:49.090
recovery, and succeed feedback.

00:07:49.766 --> 00:07:54.279
The block library may own cards,
accordion tabs, carousels, and buttons,

00:07:54.961 --> 00:08:00.460
and the configuration makes expose or
hide control that allow the editors

00:08:00.484 --> 00:08:02.364
to change the structures and color.

00:08:03.066 --> 00:08:09.084
The content model may take over heading
or meaningful links easy, or may require

00:08:09.147 --> 00:08:13.557
the, that every editor understand the
semantic structure without support.

00:08:14.654 --> 00:08:19.311
And by the time the first custom ticket
enter development, the project may

00:08:19.369 --> 00:08:24.486
already contain most of the patterns
that will determine its accessibility.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:28.871
So this change the question we ask
at the beginning of the project.

00:08:29.397 --> 00:08:34.869
Instead of asking, "Can this tool
produce the design?" We also ask,

00:08:35.247 --> 00:08:40.885
"Which user behavior will this tool
control, and what the default will

00:08:41.134 --> 00:08:46.944
establish across the site?" So the
earlier a choice become a shared default,

00:08:47.314 --> 00:08:49.710
the larger is if the future impact.

00:08:50.275 --> 00:08:52.144
A local defect is expensive.

00:08:52.611 --> 00:08:55.498
A defective default is
an architecture problem.

00:08:56.209 --> 00:08:59.714
A theme is often evaluated
as a visual package.

00:09:00.101 --> 00:09:05.221
We inspect typography, spacing,
templates, color options,

00:09:05.312 --> 00:09:06.913
WooCommerce forms, and performance.

00:09:07.449 --> 00:09:11.513
We click through the demo and
decide whether it feel modern.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:16.009
But a theme is also an interaction
and structured package.

00:09:16.682 --> 00:09:21.082
It might generate the header and
navigation on every public page.

00:09:21.683 --> 00:09:24.807
It might decide whether
a skip link exists.

00:09:25.218 --> 00:09:28.790
It might control which element
become the main landmark.

00:09:29.278 --> 00:09:31.673
It might style every link and button.

00:09:32.256 --> 00:09:36.268
Also, it might determine how menus
change at a smaller viewport.

00:09:36.779 --> 00:09:40.900
It might provide the search overlay,
the comment form, pagination,

00:09:41.194 --> 00:09:44.206
archive heading, and error template.

00:09:45.290 --> 00:09:49.559
This is why the WordPress documentation
tell them… the tell the theme

00:09:49.635 --> 00:09:54.509
authors to consider accessibility from
the start of the project because the

00:09:54.514 --> 00:10:00.449
specification is say like rather to
attend last minute adjustment, just

00:10:00.691 --> 00:10:02.559
go directly to the accessibility.

00:10:03.069 --> 00:10:07.589
And also the current WordPress
accessibility requirement also

00:10:07.591 --> 00:10:09.550
make the important distinction.

00:10:10.218 --> 00:10:14.525
The tags apply to the theme
foundation, not to the complete site.

00:10:15.191 --> 00:10:20.148
The final site still include content,
plugins, configuration, and customization.

00:10:20.992 --> 00:10:27.381
The distinction is central to this
talk because a theme can satisfy, um,

00:10:27.393 --> 00:10:33.836
the, the own review criteria and still
participate in an inaccessible final

00:10:33.925 --> 00:10:37.107
experience after other layer modified.

00:10:37.979 --> 00:10:41.238
So the theme evaluation
therefore need two more views.

00:10:41.856 --> 00:10:46.605
The first one is the isolated
themes, and we need to ask like,

00:10:46.993 --> 00:10:49.106
what does it generate by default?

00:10:49.541 --> 00:10:51.736
And the second is the intended stack.

00:10:52.389 --> 00:10:58.423
Um, you should ask like, what happen
when the required plugins, the imported

00:10:58.424 --> 00:11:02.501
starter content, the customizer,
and the configuration are active?

00:11:03.278 --> 00:11:09.284
A theme that work in isolation but
require an inaccessibility companion

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:10.991
plugin is not a safe foundation.

00:11:11.584 --> 00:11:17.384
In fact, the WordPress accessibility, uh,
guidance update is, it, it ex-explicitly

00:11:17.384 --> 00:11:23.064
say that theme using the tag must not
be recommended or required plugin that

00:11:23.103 --> 00:11:25.753
introduce the no accessibility barriers.

00:11:26.944 --> 00:11:29.578
That requirement recognize
a practical truth.

00:11:30.370 --> 00:11:32.310
Set two parts are part of the product.

00:11:32.893 --> 00:11:37.325
The bug can be born during
onboarding before the site

00:11:37.379 --> 00:11:39.053
owner create the first page.

00:11:39.685 --> 00:11:43.179
Plugins are usually selected
through feature comparison.

00:11:43.867 --> 00:11:47.629
Like for example, we start asking
for every plugin we install, like

00:11:47.635 --> 00:11:51.641
for example, does booking plugin
support recurring appointment?

00:11:52.064 --> 00:11:55.686
Does, uh, form plugin
connect with the CRM?

00:11:56.179 --> 00:12:00.515
Uh, does the commerce extension
support the payment provider?

00:12:01.021 --> 00:12:04.001
Does the pop-up tool
include the targeting rules?

00:12:04.761 --> 00:12:09.007
Those are… question are
necessary, but they don't reveal the

00:12:09.041 --> 00:12:11.378
accessibility risk of the dependency.

00:12:12.243 --> 00:12:16.612
A useful way to qualify the plugins
is not by the popularity or the

00:12:16.612 --> 00:12:21.624
size of the plugin, but by the
user behavior the plugin control.

00:12:22.463 --> 00:12:26.939
A backup plugin might be
operationally critical because it

00:12:26.987 --> 00:12:31.311
contribute, uh, uh, very little
to the public interaction model.

00:12:32.009 --> 00:12:38.156
But if we are using a book-- a booking
plugin may generate only a few screen.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:42.412
But those screen also control the
date selection, the validation,

00:12:42.455 --> 00:12:44.794
confirmation, and probably the payment.

00:12:45.527 --> 00:12:52.157
So, uh, also, if we use a small cookie
banner, this can be appear before every

00:12:52.158 --> 00:12:55.137
other task and obstruct the entire site.

00:12:55.936 --> 00:13:00.907
A chat widget, widget can inject
its own focus behavior and update

00:13:01.015 --> 00:13:05.434
in, uh, also update independently
for the WordPress release process.

00:13:06.423 --> 00:13:11.499
So the highest risk plugin are
generally those that generate

00:13:11.511 --> 00:13:17.074
interaction, like forms, checkout,
authentication, calendars, dialogues,

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:22.682
tabs, accordion search, filtering,
table, and embedding services.

00:13:23.173 --> 00:13:27.407
So the goal is not to perform
a complete conformance audit

00:13:27.424 --> 00:13:29.261
before installing anything.

00:13:29.609 --> 00:13:33.433
So the goal is a technical due diligence.

00:13:34.161 --> 00:13:40.051
For each high-risk dependency, we need
to identify the critical behavior that

00:13:40.082 --> 00:13:41.642
is what's selected by the provider.

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:48.113
Um, this examine, uh, the default output,
the av-available configuration, the

00:13:48.122 --> 00:13:53.936
ability to override template safely, the
vendor's update model, and the escalation

00:13:53.963 --> 00:13:56.530
path when a defect exists upstream.

00:13:57.297 --> 00:14:01.638
Uh, this avoid the common trap,
building the entire project around

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:06.870
a dependency and discovering late
that the only way to a correct and

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:11.935
fundamental interaction is to fork the
plugin, maintain a fragile override,

00:14:12.235 --> 00:14:17.491
or replacing it after the content and
business process already depend on it.

00:14:17.933 --> 00:14:24.657
So- Accessibility bugs are born
when feature fits, it's evaluated,

00:14:24.692 --> 00:14:27.077
and integration quality is assumed.

00:14:27.733 --> 00:14:31.065
The block editor makes reuse
a first-class capability.

00:14:31.489 --> 00:14:35.793
A team can create patterns, synced
pattern, template part, custom

00:14:35.795 --> 00:14:37.723
block, and editor restriction.

00:14:38.075 --> 00:14:41.943
This is a major opportunity for
accessibility because the system

00:14:41.945 --> 00:14:46.972
can encode good decision once and
making them available everywhere.

00:14:47.553 --> 00:14:49.237
It's also a multiplier for defect.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:54.895
A custom card block that produce unclear
link does not create an unclear link.

00:14:55.195 --> 00:14:59.277
It create the pattern every
time an editor insert the block.

00:14:59.913 --> 00:15:03.019
A call to action pattern that
use hidden labels for visual

00:15:03.021 --> 00:15:08.193
side can spread inconsistent
structure across campaign pages.

00:15:09.113 --> 00:15:11.638
A shared pattern have
an even bigger impact.

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.193
WordPress updated everywhere it is used.

00:15:14.861 --> 00:15:18.933
That's a great when the pattern is
correct, but if the pattern has a problem,

00:15:19.262 --> 00:15:21.572
the problem show up everywhere, too.

00:15:22.145 --> 00:15:26.794
The same is true of template
parts such a header or footer.

00:15:27.091 --> 00:15:30.472
One modification can affect
every template that use them.

00:15:31.196 --> 00:15:35.214
This lead to a practical rule:
review the generating pattern before

00:15:35.222 --> 00:15:37.776
reviewing dozens of generated page.

00:15:38.414 --> 00:15:41.389
WordPress also provide control
that protect good default.

00:15:41.793 --> 00:15:47.150
Block locking and content only editing
can preserve a tested structure while

00:15:47.214 --> 00:15:49.607
allowing others to change the content.

00:15:50.063 --> 00:15:54.344
Allowed block list can reduce the
number of uncontrolled component

00:15:54.373 --> 00:15:57.265
available in a specific editing content.

00:15:57.967 --> 00:16:01.374
Global style can constrain
palettes and typography.

00:16:02.045 --> 00:16:06.901
These capabilities should not be used to
remove the necessary editorial freedom.

00:16:07.559 --> 00:16:12.171
They should be used to prevent the content
team from having to solve structural

00:16:12.217 --> 00:16:14.921
engineering problems on every page.

00:16:15.774 --> 00:16:21.088
A mature WordPress project asks two
question about every reusable asset.

00:16:21.654 --> 00:16:23.734
What does this pattern make easy?

00:16:25.127 --> 00:16:29.512
And what mistake will I repeat
if its default is wrong?

00:16:30.391 --> 00:16:34.606
Even when a theme or plugin begins
with a good default, WordPress

00:16:34.654 --> 00:16:37.658
allow the later layers to modify it.

00:16:38.185 --> 00:16:41.649
The official theme documentation
describe the hierarchy for

00:16:41.682 --> 00:16:43.736
global settings and style.

00:16:44.512 --> 00:16:46.382
WordPress provide the default.

00:16:46.864 --> 00:16:48.968
A theme also can override them.

00:16:49.323 --> 00:16:51.946
A child theme can override the parent.

00:16:52.514 --> 00:16:58.063
The user configuration saved through the
site editor can take the pri-priority,

00:16:58.495 --> 00:17:03.011
and plugins and server-side filters
can also modify the final data.

00:17:03.823 --> 00:17:06.446
This flexibility is one
of the WordPress strength.

00:17:07.087 --> 00:17:11.282
It's also why reviewing a
repository doesn't always

00:17:11.292 --> 00:17:12.898
reveal the delivery behavior.

00:17:13.459 --> 00:17:18.555
A developer can inspect the parent
theme and find a visible focus style.

00:17:19.124 --> 00:17:24.822
A user-level global style may change
may… or override the relevant colors.

00:17:25.479 --> 00:17:28.518
A child theme may remove the outline.

00:17:28.861 --> 00:17:31.494
A plugin may inject
more specific selector.

00:17:32.002 --> 00:17:36.581
The custom CSS, if it's stored
in the database, may win

00:17:36.595 --> 00:17:38.129
over all this configuration.

00:17:38.634 --> 00:17:41.344
So the same applies to
the outside of the theme.

00:17:42.076 --> 00:17:45.174
A form plugin can
generate a visible label.

00:17:45.665 --> 00:17:48.210
A theme option can hide
that l- that label.

00:17:48.763 --> 00:17:52.563
Uh, a page builder can wrap the
field in an additional markup.

00:17:53.594 --> 00:17:56.108
The JavaScript can replace
the native control.

00:17:56.641 --> 00:18:01.016
The final browser output is the
real result and the, the complete

00:18:01.029 --> 00:18:02.612
configuration of this chain.

00:18:03.236 --> 00:18:07.002
This is especially important when
you have an existing project.

00:18:07.793 --> 00:18:10.846
The current thing might own
the Git repository, but not

00:18:10.857 --> 00:18:12.203
the database configuration.

00:18:12.767 --> 00:18:16.874
It's my own, uh, custom code,
but not the vendor dependency.

00:18:17.445 --> 00:18:22.268
It's my own, the theme, but not
the content part that the editor

00:18:22.497 --> 00:18:24.323
have been duplicated for years.

00:18:24.913 --> 00:18:30.315
So this bug is born wherever the
behavior is felt made unsafe.

00:18:30.888 --> 00:18:35.871
Finding that point requires more than
reading the last file that you changed.

00:18:36.524 --> 00:18:41.845
An inherited WordPress project add one
more problem, history without context.

00:18:42.365 --> 00:18:46.006
The theme see the current output,
but it doesn't know why the

00:18:46.018 --> 00:18:47.578
system look and behave that way.

00:18:48.287 --> 00:18:53.028
A rule might be have added to solve
a browser issue five years ago.

00:18:53.475 --> 00:18:56.895
A plugin might be frozen
because a template override

00:18:57.224 --> 00:18:59.074
depends on the old markup.

00:18:59.591 --> 00:19:04.652
A block might be deprecated, but still
it sits across hundreds of posts.

00:19:05.288 --> 00:19:10.103
A custom JavaScript file might
compensate all this lack of the

00:19:10.236 --> 00:19:12.172
limitation that you see inside the theme.

00:19:12.677 --> 00:19:17.863
So the wrong first move is to
treat every visible defect as

00:19:17.865 --> 00:19:20.356
an independent page problem.

00:19:21.294 --> 00:19:26.943
The better first move that we can do
is to have a map of all the generators.

00:19:27.936 --> 00:19:33.300
So once we identify the active parent,
like we can go to the parent theme, the

00:19:33.309 --> 00:19:39.153
child theme, we can see all the custom
plugins, the all the most used plugins,

00:19:39.487 --> 00:19:45.247
the code snippet tool, the templates
override, global CSS, site editor changes,

00:19:45.578 --> 00:19:51.147
saved patterns, page builder templates,
and also for the third-party script.

00:19:51.736 --> 00:19:57.136
Then we identify which of those layers
control each of the critical interactions.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:04.024
These do not require documenting the
entire installation before work begin.

00:20:04.466 --> 00:20:07.997
It require enough shifting
knowledge to distinguish an

00:20:08.005 --> 00:20:09.908
in-instance from the source.

00:20:10.775 --> 00:20:16.627
So if one page have a wrong heading, the
source may be the content, not the code.

00:20:17.103 --> 00:20:23.034
And if every page, if using the same
pattern, have the wrong heading, then

00:20:23.049 --> 00:20:28.123
the configuration probably is a plugin
or probably is a theme st-style.

00:20:28.487 --> 00:20:34.527
So if every interaction element lost,
uh, the, the focus indication, the,

00:20:34.622 --> 00:20:38.511
the source probably a global, uh,
configuration we have in the theme.

00:20:39.054 --> 00:20:44.368
So accessibility remediation become
much more durable when the team ask

00:20:44.814 --> 00:20:50.637
what generated this output before
asking what CSS can hide the symptoms.

00:20:51.431 --> 00:20:55.675
A browser does not present the
user with separate theme, plugins,

00:20:55.706 --> 00:20:57.165
block, and content experience.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:20:59.094
It present one page.

00:20:59.781 --> 00:21:04.022
That page may contain a core
navigation, block styled by the

00:21:04.043 --> 00:21:09.882
theme, extended by the plugin, placed
inside a template part, edited through

00:21:09.884 --> 00:21:14.040
the site editor, and supplemented
by the third party chat widget.

00:21:14.789 --> 00:21:18.272
This is why component level
claims are not enough.

00:21:19.058 --> 00:21:24.072
The theme author can correctly state that
the theme provide a valid foundation.

00:21:24.583 --> 00:21:28.286
The plugin author can correctly
state that the plugin default

00:21:28.315 --> 00:21:30.261
markup include the label.

00:21:30.908 --> 00:21:34.772
The agency can correctly state
that, uh, the custom block

00:21:34.772 --> 00:21:36.482
follow the project convention.

00:21:37.157 --> 00:21:42.389
The content editor can correctly state
that the page match the approved layout.

00:21:43.145 --> 00:21:45.331
The combined output can still fail.

00:21:46.107 --> 00:21:51.546
The correct unit of accountability is
not only the component in isolation.

00:21:52.163 --> 00:21:55.933
Is it the behavior of
the rendered integration?

00:21:57.122 --> 00:22:01.504
This doesn't mean every team
must own every vendor's code.

00:22:01.596 --> 00:22:06.224
It's mean the site owner and the
delivery team must own, own the

00:22:06.225 --> 00:22:12.380
decision to include, configure,
override, replace, uh, or constrains

00:22:12.432 --> 00:22:14.328
the dependency in the final product.

00:22:15.185 --> 00:22:19.013
WordPress multiply accessibility
bugs because it's make

00:22:19.047 --> 00:22:21.457
composition and reuse easy.

00:22:22.233 --> 00:22:27.040
Those same strength also give, give
us the best place to solve them and

00:22:27.062 --> 00:22:32.301
also repair, uh, the generating layer
instead of the visible instance.

00:22:32.511 --> 00:22:37.197
A useful diagnostic shift is to
stop asking only which component

00:22:37.291 --> 00:22:42.278
is inaccessible, and ask which
integration between components

00:22:42.551 --> 00:22:44.262
make the final behavior unsafe?

00:22:45.181 --> 00:22:48.000
Consider a form plugin that
generate a visible label.

00:22:48.736 --> 00:22:53.721
The team include a utility class that
visually hide label for a cleaner design.

00:22:54.644 --> 00:22:57.744
A project configuration applies
that class to every field.

00:22:58.354 --> 00:23:01.091
The plugin is not m- missing any label.

00:23:01.999 --> 00:23:04.512
The theme is not necessarily
broken in installation.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:09.015
The integration decision remove
the user-facing information.

00:23:09.882 --> 00:23:12.532
Consider a native button
generated by code.

00:23:13.294 --> 00:23:15.732
The theme define a visible focus style.

00:23:16.269 --> 00:23:21.857
Later, a global CSS reset remove
outline from all the controls.

00:23:22.465 --> 00:23:24.244
The markup remain correct.

00:23:24.768 --> 00:23:30.978
The site-wide visual state become unusable
for people who rely on focus indicator.

00:23:32.575 --> 00:23:36.401
Consider a plugin that generate
valid success mes-messages.

00:23:37.123 --> 00:23:39.893
Custom JavaScript replace
the plugin submission flow

00:23:40.127 --> 00:23:44.134
and insert a visual identical
message in a different container.

00:23:45.053 --> 00:23:48.299
The business event succeed, but
the feedback behavior change.

00:23:49.741 --> 00:23:51.655
These are the composition defects.

00:23:52.662 --> 00:23:56.577
They are difficult to locate through
the ownership boundaries because every

00:23:56.578 --> 00:24:01.348
contributor can show that its own
layer worked under different condition.

00:24:02.042 --> 00:24:06.415
The final page is a contract
between markup, styles, JavaScript,

00:24:06.812 --> 00:24:12.258
configuration, content, browser
behavior, and assistive technology.

00:24:12.932 --> 00:24:17.747
When any layer silently change
the contract, the defect appears

00:24:17.805 --> 00:24:19.389
at the integration boundaries.

00:24:19.977 --> 00:24:24.300
This is why the correct repair often
require collaboration across roles

00:24:24.588 --> 00:24:29.411
rather than sending every accessibility
issue to the front end developer.

00:24:29.971 --> 00:24:35.485
Accessibility ownership becomes confused
because teams use one word, owner, for

00:24:35.492 --> 00:24:38.124
several different responsibilities.

00:24:38.798 --> 00:24:42.847
The source owner is the person or
vendor able to change the code or

00:24:42.875 --> 00:24:44.636
configuration that generate the defect.

00:24:45.227 --> 00:24:50.342
The task owner is the owner
responsible for business outcome.

00:24:50.788 --> 00:24:56.007
For example, e-commerce team owns,
uh, checkout completion even when a

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:58.018
payment plugin provide the interface.

00:24:58.697 --> 00:25:03.558
The release owner has authority
to decide whether the remaining

00:25:03.558 --> 00:25:05.420
risk is acceptable for production.

00:25:06.157 --> 00:25:07.498
This role might be different.

00:25:08.114 --> 00:25:10.326
A plugin vendor may own the source code.

00:25:10.644 --> 00:25:13.244
The agency may own the integration.

00:25:13.259 --> 00:25:15.126
The client may own the business task.

00:25:15.700 --> 00:25:19.139
A product manager may
own the release priority.

00:25:19.618 --> 00:25:22.978
A content team may own the page
where the problem becomes visible.

00:25:23.785 --> 00:25:28.018
When those roles are not distinguished,
the issue move between the teams.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:33.194
The agency say, "The bug belongs to the
plugin." The vendor say, "The themes

00:25:33.235 --> 00:25:37.856
modify the output." The team developer
say, "Custom CSS can cause, cause the

00:25:37.867 --> 00:25:44.320
conflict." The client say, "The agency
is responsible for the website." So

00:25:44.359 --> 00:25:48.918
every statement may contain part of
the truth, but the user remains broken.

00:25:49.275 --> 00:25:54.064
Research on accessibility in software
practice have repeatedly found that

00:25:54.406 --> 00:25:59.329
organizational factors, unclear
requirement, limited resources, and

00:25:59.339 --> 00:26:04.360
difference between roles strongly
affect all the responsibility outcome.

00:26:05.088 --> 00:26:10.720
All the planning guidance, they like
recommend assigning responsibility across

00:26:10.725 --> 00:26:16.742
the production process instead of treating
accessibility as a developer only task.

00:26:17.588 --> 00:26:22.369
The operational solution is not
to assign every issue to the…

00:26:22.639 --> 00:26:24.788
that one accessibility specialist.

00:26:25.816 --> 00:26:28.922
It is, it is to recall
actually form ownership.

00:26:29.311 --> 00:26:33.786
Who can change the, the source
code, who owns the user journey,

00:26:33.969 --> 00:26:35.689
and who has the release authority.

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:40.434
This structure make escalation
possible even when the code

00:26:40.437 --> 00:26:42.269
live outside the organization.

00:26:42.672 --> 00:26:46.670
We now have content to follow one
defect through another workflow.

00:26:47.449 --> 00:26:50.267
The fictional project is a
health service WordPress site.

00:26:50.982 --> 00:26:54.459
The important user outcome
is a booking appointment.

00:26:55.801 --> 00:26:58.657
The team provide navigation
and page template.

00:26:59.447 --> 00:27:02.720
Our form, our form plugin generate
the fields and validation.

00:27:03.361 --> 00:27:06.740
Custom code adds a confirmation
dialog before submission.

00:27:07.615 --> 00:27:11.316
An integration send the
appointment to external CRM.

00:27:12.062 --> 00:27:14.100
A success notice confirm completion.

00:27:14.936 --> 00:27:19.821
The team use a familiar delivery process,
requirement, des-design, implementation,

00:27:20.048 --> 00:27:23.057
staging, QA, and the scheduled release.

00:27:23.886 --> 00:27:25.013
The defect is simple.

00:27:25.434 --> 00:27:29.310
The confirmation step appears
correctly and submit correctly,

00:27:29.649 --> 00:27:35.280
but the transition into and in and
out of that step is in-incomplete.

00:27:35.963 --> 00:27:40.337
It also disconnect some error
feedback from the or-original form.

00:27:40.795 --> 00:27:43.637
The specific dialog is not
the subject of this chapter.

00:27:44.467 --> 00:27:51.378
It is only the example that let us see how
the meaning of complete becomes narrower

00:27:51.461 --> 00:27:53.449
and the feature move through the process.

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:58.365
The discovery document includes
the sentence, "A new appointment

00:27:58.514 --> 00:28:00.201
flow should be accessible."

00:28:01.286 --> 00:28:05.447
That sentence communicate intent,
but it doesn't transfer behavior

00:28:05.688 --> 00:28:06.882
of the rest of the workflow.

00:28:07.833 --> 00:28:12.636
The implementation ticket said, "Display
a confirmation modal after the user

00:28:12.641 --> 00:28:18.069
select book appointment." This included
text, buttons, labels, and screenshot.

00:28:18.844 --> 00:28:22.295
The developer can satisfy every
writing requirement by making

00:28:22.609 --> 00:28:26.197
central container vis-visible
and connecting the confirmation

00:28:26.199 --> 00:28:27.667
button to the submission function.

00:28:28.784 --> 00:28:31.617
The missing behavior were never rejected.

00:28:32.062 --> 00:28:33.212
They were never requested.

00:28:33.993 --> 00:28:38.096
A stronger requirement doesn't
need to quote a standard or

00:28:38.149 --> 00:28:40.241
contain a long checklist.

00:28:40.956 --> 00:28:45.050
It need to describe the user
outcome across the stage change.

00:28:45.470 --> 00:28:49.687
For example, we can write like, "When
the user review the appointment,

00:28:49.767 --> 00:28:53.090
the confirmation appears without
losing their place in the form.

00:28:53.632 --> 00:28:57.191
The confirmation can be
operated and dismissed through

00:28:57.226 --> 00:28:59.049
the supported input method.

00:28:59.692 --> 00:29:02.868
Closing it return the user
to a predictable point.

00:29:03.525 --> 00:29:08.882
If submission fail, the relevant error may
remain connected to the original fields.

00:29:09.492 --> 00:29:14.306
If submission succeed, completion
is communicated without requiring

00:29:14.313 --> 00:29:15.817
the users to search the page."

00:29:16.595 --> 00:29:21.926
So this requirement is useful to design,
development, review, QA, and release.

00:29:22.721 --> 00:29:25.749
The failure at this stage
is not lacking of goodwill.

00:29:26.104 --> 00:29:27.897
It's a loss of specificity.

00:29:28.761 --> 00:29:32.818
When accessibility is stored as a
general non-functional statement,

00:29:33.216 --> 00:29:39.075
each downstream role must reinterpret
and independently… The design may

00:29:39.088 --> 00:29:44.973
interpret it as a contract, development
may interpret it as semantic HTML, QA

00:29:44.991 --> 00:29:47.147
may interpret it as a running scanner.

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:52.278
None of these, uh, interpretation
necessarily describe the complete task.

00:29:53.147 --> 00:29:57.053
This will survive the requirement
because the behavior that will

00:29:57.066 --> 00:30:00.304
reveal it never become part of
the definition of the feature.

00:30:00.906 --> 00:30:02.951
The design file is professional prepared.

00:30:03.545 --> 00:30:06.784
It shows the appointment form,
it shows the confirmation dialog,

00:30:07.069 --> 00:30:08.601
it shows the succeed state.

00:30:08.962 --> 00:30:12.516
It includes a responsive
layout, color, spacing, button

00:30:12.517 --> 00:30:14.619
hierarchy, and error style.

00:30:15.256 --> 00:30:17.455
The screen are visually complete.

00:30:17.952 --> 00:30:19.341
The transition contract is not.

00:30:19.943 --> 00:30:23.068
The design does not describe
what happened to the user content

00:30:23.598 --> 00:30:25.180
when the confirmation opens.

00:30:25.467 --> 00:30:28.905
It does not indicate whether
the background remains active.

00:30:29.270 --> 00:30:32.495
It does not describe how the
user exits the confirmation

00:30:32.500 --> 00:30:34.189
without completing the action.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:38.969
It doesn't show what happens when the
external CRM rejects the appointment.

00:30:39.413 --> 00:30:43.848
It doesn't in- it does, does indicate
whether the success message should

00:30:43.848 --> 00:30:48.773
replace the form, appear above it, or is
announcing why the user remained in place.

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:52.355
These are not implemented,
implementation trivia.

00:30:52.867 --> 00:30:56.794
They determine whether the
sequence is under- understandable.

00:30:57.785 --> 00:31:00.649
The design tools not only
pres-represent state.

00:31:01.020 --> 00:31:04.176
Accessibility defects often
appear during transition.

00:31:04.464 --> 00:31:09.441
Opening, closing, loading, validating,
replacing, redirecting, and recovery.

00:31:10.244 --> 00:31:13.795
A better design handoff include a
small interaction annotation for

00:31:13.851 --> 00:31:15.619
every meaningful state change.

00:31:16.157 --> 00:31:17.011
What triggered the change?

00:31:17.428 --> 00:31:18.436
What become active?

00:31:18.463 --> 00:31:19.496
What become inactive?

00:31:19.739 --> 00:31:21.406
What information must be communicated?

00:31:22.329 --> 00:31:25.284
Where the, the… Where
can the user continue?

00:31:25.806 --> 00:31:29.229
This is not, it's, it's like asking
the designer to write JavaScript.

00:31:29.485 --> 00:31:33.360
It's not asking the design to
specify the experience rather

00:31:33.387 --> 00:31:34.902
than only that the pixel.

00:31:35.639 --> 00:31:40.338
This bug survive the design because
every individual frame looks

00:31:40.341 --> 00:31:44.705
correct while the relationship
between frames remain undefined.

00:31:45.156 --> 00:31:46.940
The feature moves to staging.

00:31:47.781 --> 00:31:49.698
QA follow the acceptance criteria.

00:31:50.074 --> 00:31:54.450
The tester complete the form, open the
confirmation, submit the appointment,

00:31:54.502 --> 00:31:55.910
and see the success message.

00:31:56.649 --> 00:31:59.017
The documented business pa-part worked.

00:31:59.395 --> 00:32:00.324
The ticket is passed.

00:32:01.051 --> 00:32:02.753
This is not necessarily poor QA.

00:32:03.004 --> 00:32:08.011
QA cannot rely verifying a behavioral
contract that never was written or

00:32:08.011 --> 00:32:10.014
represented in the material it received.

00:32:10.628 --> 00:32:14.701
If the expected results say, "The
confirmation appear and the appointment

00:32:14.726 --> 00:32:19.993
is created," the tester can complete
the expectation exactly why the

00:32:20.004 --> 00:32:23.980
accessibility defect remain outside
of the definition of the succeed.

00:32:24.782 --> 00:32:30.072
Adding a va-vague final instruction
such as check accessibility

00:32:30.301 --> 00:32:31.446
does not solve the problem.

00:32:31.878 --> 00:32:36.848
It asks QA to reconstruct at the
end of the process behavior that

00:32:37.014 --> 00:32:38.359
earlier state never defined.

00:32:39.646 --> 00:32:43.896
The stronger approach is to place the
specific behavior inside the acceptance

00:32:43.903 --> 00:32:46.288
criteria and the feature itself.

00:32:46.872 --> 00:32:50.660
Then QA is not performing a
separate accessibility ritual.

00:32:50.936 --> 00:32:55.937
It's verifying the same product behavior
the product committed to deliver, and

00:32:56.063 --> 00:33:01.446
then this, the bug survived QA because the
acceptance criteria describe the visible

00:33:01.466 --> 00:33:06.231
business result, but omit the conditions
required for the complete experience.

00:33:06.972 --> 00:33:10.620
Shortly before release, someone's
noticed that the confirmation

00:33:10.620 --> 00:33:12.257
behavior is incomplete.

00:33:12.867 --> 00:33:13.939
The issue is real.

00:33:14.271 --> 00:33:16.083
The team agreed that it should be fixed.

00:33:16.692 --> 00:33:17.851
It's added to the backlog.

00:33:18.422 --> 00:33:20.149
The release is still proceed.

00:33:20.718 --> 00:33:24.316
This is where the ownership
and authority separates.

00:33:25.348 --> 00:33:29.452
A developer may have the responsibility
of repairing the source, but not

00:33:29.466 --> 00:33:31.863
the authority to delay the launch.

00:33:32.473 --> 00:33:35.866
QA may have the authority to fail
a documented requirement, but

00:33:35.866 --> 00:33:37.850
not an undocumented behavior.

00:33:38.492 --> 00:33:42.364
And accessibility, especially,
may explain the user impact,

00:33:42.378 --> 00:33:45.195
but function only as an advisor.

00:33:46.228 --> 00:33:51.500
A product manager may own the schedule,
but l- have the lack of severity

00:33:51.505 --> 00:33:57.576
model that distinguish a minor, minor
issue from a task-blocking barrier.

00:33:58.368 --> 00:34:01.844
Without a shared release policy,
the decision default to schedule

00:34:01.902 --> 00:34:06.344
is a pressure, so the organization
needs to clear the answer to

00:34:06.344 --> 00:34:07.989
three real simple questions.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:09.999
Who classified the severity?

00:34:10.268 --> 00:34:12.289
Who owns the affected user task?

00:34:12.642 --> 00:34:15.253
Who can block or
explicitly accept the risk?

00:34:16.438 --> 00:34:21.277
This doesn't mean that every accessibility
i- is finding like a stop of…

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:22.605
for every release.

00:34:22.985 --> 00:34:27.354
It mean the decision is made
deliberately using impact, reach,

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:32.110
available alternative, and whether the
current release introduce the barrier.

00:34:33.453 --> 00:34:37.922
Again, the bug survived release when
everyone can report the problem,

00:34:37.936 --> 00:34:43.131
but nobody have that definite
authority to add as he own severity.

00:34:43.800 --> 00:34:47.631
The root cause is not simply that
somebody forgot an accessibility rule.

00:34:48.187 --> 00:34:49.907
The root cause is contract loss.

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:54.269
The intended outcome entered the
project as a broad intention.

00:34:54.659 --> 00:34:57.481
It became a ticket center,
not visible object.

00:34:57.829 --> 00:35:00.921
It became a design made
of isolated screen.

00:35:01.555 --> 00:35:06.274
The implementation inherit those
omission, QA then never verify the

00:35:06.275 --> 00:35:10.774
result describing the, those, those
material, and release have no explicit

00:35:10.774 --> 00:35:12.774
rule for acting on the remaining risk.

00:35:13.520 --> 00:35:17.961
Each hand off that shared definition
of the feature become narrower.

00:35:18.824 --> 00:35:22.234
A stronger workflow must
preserve the necessary behavior

00:35:22.283 --> 00:35:24.770
in form that each role can use.

00:35:25.253 --> 00:35:28.558
This is why the final chapter
focus on the behavior requirement.

00:35:29.169 --> 00:35:33.106
Reusable default, explicit
ownership, release authority,

00:35:33.326 --> 00:35:37.254
and protection against regression
when the WordPress stack changes.

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:42.426
The solution is not a large
accessibility phase added at the end.

00:35:42.756 --> 00:35:47.036
It's preventing the behavior from
disappearing as the work moves forward.

00:35:47.521 --> 00:35:50.974
After release, the team receive
a report and respond quickly.

00:35:51.495 --> 00:35:54.613
A developer adds page specific CSS.

00:35:55.036 --> 00:35:57.310
All small script move the message.

00:35:57.671 --> 00:36:00.354
The reported page now appear to work.

00:36:00.859 --> 00:36:01.684
The ticket is closed.

00:36:02.307 --> 00:36:06.711
But the shared form template
still generate the same structure.

00:36:06.942 --> 00:36:10.202
The global theme rule still
apply to every other forms.

00:36:10.815 --> 00:36:12.539
New pages can reproduce a problem.

00:36:12.575 --> 00:36:16.103
Existing pages outside the
report may remain unchanged.

00:36:16.863 --> 00:36:21.134
This is the difference between
an instance fix and a source fix.

00:36:21.971 --> 00:36:24.619
The instance fixes are
sometimes necessary.

00:36:25.187 --> 00:36:29.635
A critical user barrier may
require an immediate patch while

00:36:29.635 --> 00:36:31.369
the durable repair is prepared.

00:36:32.025 --> 00:36:36.360
The mistake is treating the emergency
patch as the final architecture.

00:36:37.364 --> 00:36:40.380
A WordPress site can make
instance patch very easy.

00:36:40.782 --> 00:36:43.197
Custom CSS can be added quickly.

00:36:43.496 --> 00:36:45.980
A code snippet can target one page ID.

00:36:46.289 --> 00:36:47.578
A template can be duplicated.

00:36:47.987 --> 00:36:50.508
A plugin setting can be
changed for one form.

00:36:51.291 --> 00:36:55.539
Those mechanic are useful, but they
can hide the generated defect and

00:36:55.819 --> 00:37:00.430
create a maintenance layer that
the next team doesn't understand.

00:37:01.213 --> 00:37:06.808
So the same bug return because the
system was never taught a safer default.

00:37:07.229 --> 00:37:11.102
Even a repair applied at the code
layer can regress if the project

00:37:11.134 --> 00:37:13.524
doesn't protect it through changes.

00:37:14.814 --> 00:37:17.740
WordPress evolved through
more than a feature release.

00:37:18.350 --> 00:37:23.839
Core change, theme change, plugin change,
administrator can enable automatic

00:37:23.899 --> 00:37:26.488
update for individual themes and plugins.

00:37:26.933 --> 00:37:32.093
Global style stored in database can change
independently, uh, of the repository.

00:37:32.448 --> 00:37:37.491
Editors can create new content, sync
patterns, update everywhere they are used.

00:37:37.941 --> 00:37:42.276
Third-party widget can change remotely
without a WordPress deployment.

00:37:42.595 --> 00:37:47.899
So a custom plugin template override may
start matching the, the vendor new markup.

00:37:48.334 --> 00:37:51.698
A CSS selector may no longer
reach the generated element.

00:37:52.464 --> 00:37:55.223
A theme updated may
change a shared component.

00:37:55.705 --> 00:37:59.705
An editor may detach a pattern
and create a local variation.

00:37:59.848 --> 00:38:03.037
A new plugin may introduce a
competing style or a script.

00:38:03.830 --> 00:38:08.678
The business feature may continue to work
while the accessibility behavior regress.

00:38:08.923 --> 00:38:10.250
Nothing dramatic need to fail.

00:38:10.692 --> 00:38:14.937
The page may still load, the form
may still submit, and the integration

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:16.348
may still receive the data.

00:38:17.339 --> 00:38:21.263
The important point is that WordPress
continually recompose the final product

00:38:21.633 --> 00:38:26.089
from changing version, configuration,
content, and external services.

00:38:26.645 --> 00:38:29.957
The an- the answer is
not to freeze the stack.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:36.519
The answer is to recognize which shared
behavior is a high risk change can alter

00:38:37.022 --> 00:38:39.026
and make part of the update decision.

00:38:39.584 --> 00:38:42.145
The workflow bends differently
depending on the project.

00:38:42.848 --> 00:38:46.772
For a new WordPress project,
qualify the theme and the high-risk

00:38:46.817 --> 00:38:49.500
plugin before site depends on them.

00:38:50.256 --> 00:38:53.749
Define the requirement behavior for
the component the project will repeat.

00:38:54.352 --> 00:38:58.464
Establish safe patterns and default
before large-scale content production.

00:38:59.098 --> 00:39:02.513
Assign ownership and decide
which failure can block release.

00:39:03.247 --> 00:39:07.243
Connect future updates to the
behavior does dependencies control.

00:39:08.051 --> 00:39:13.692
For an inherited project, maybe mapping
the generator, the themes, plugins,

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:19.156
setting patterns, custom code, content
convention, third-party services.

00:39:19.653 --> 00:39:23.439
Connect repeated effect to those
source layer before creating

00:39:23.718 --> 00:39:25.750
the page specific patches.

00:39:27.322 --> 00:39:32.393
Correct immediate barriers that change the
reusable source that can reproduce them.

00:39:33.070 --> 00:39:38.734
Assign the ownership and identify
which feature they can undo the repair.

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:43.929
The operator always ask, "Which
default are we about to distribute?"

00:39:44.595 --> 00:39:49.667
In the inherited project, we ask, "Which
layer are already generating the current

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:55.969
behavior? What approaches lead to the same
operating state?" The, the team know what

00:39:56.007 --> 00:40:00.715
generate the behavior, what the expected
behavior is, and who can change it.

00:40:01.156 --> 00:40:05.545
Also, who own the results and who can
act when the risk is not acceptable.

00:40:05.886 --> 00:40:11.067
A team can pin this work without
redesigning the entire delivery process.

00:40:11.490 --> 00:40:15.790
In the first week, map the themes,
plugin, blocks, configuration, and

00:40:15.790 --> 00:40:20.177
third-party services that control
the site most important interaction.

00:40:20.817 --> 00:40:24.723
Identify one repeated unsafe
default for one integration

00:40:24.729 --> 00:40:26.659
that regularly create defect.

00:40:27.363 --> 00:40:30.921
In the second week, take one
active feature and replace the

00:40:30.944 --> 00:40:34.719
vague accessibility statement
with behavioral requirement.

00:40:35.429 --> 00:40:40.037
Assign the source owner, the owner of
the user code, and the release authority.

00:40:40.441 --> 00:40:44.721
In the third week, correct one
shared resource, update rule,

00:40:45.013 --> 00:40:49.205
plugin interaction, uh, custom
block, pattern, or content default.

00:40:50.019 --> 00:40:55.821
Keep an emer- emergency page level
patch only when it is still needed,

00:40:56.348 --> 00:40:58.299
and document that is temporary.

00:40:59.388 --> 00:41:03.281
For the fourth week, connect one
high-risk update to a defined

00:41:03.423 --> 00:41:07.586
re-check and add one release rule
for the task blocking barrier.

00:41:08.267 --> 00:41:13.144
This produce a working change in the
delivery system, not a policy documented

00:41:13.533 --> 00:41:15.265
waiting for the future project.

00:41:16.006 --> 00:41:20.127
The objective here is straightforward:
change how the bug enters the

00:41:20.127 --> 00:41:22.163
project and how it's reproduced.

00:41:23.069 --> 00:41:28.104
Also, how it's reached the release and
how it's prevented from returning again.

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:32.278
Accessibility bugs survive modern
development workflow because each

00:41:32.341 --> 00:41:34.357
state see only part of the product.

00:41:35.101 --> 00:41:40.082
The bug is born when themes, plugin,
blocks, configuration, and content

00:41:40.102 --> 00:41:43.993
model are selected without examining
the behavior they will control.

00:41:44.834 --> 00:41:49.493
WordPress multiply it through global
style, template part, patterns, custom

00:41:49.493 --> 00:41:52.296
blocks, integration, and repeated content.

00:41:53.229 --> 00:41:57.772
The workflow let it pass when requirements
reduce behavior to appearance.

00:41:58.401 --> 00:42:03.542
Design show isolated state, QA receive
incomplete acceptance criteria,

00:42:03.817 --> 00:42:07.718
and release have the no authority
model for the remaining risk.

00:42:08.439 --> 00:42:12.531
So this is the reason the bug return
when the team patch because it's patching

00:42:12.535 --> 00:42:16.875
the visible instance, leaving the
generated layer unchanged, and treat

00:42:17.240 --> 00:42:22.421
every update as a routine maintenance
rather than change the product experience.

00:42:23.256 --> 00:42:27.506
A stronger workflow addresses
that task, task calls directly.

00:42:27.848 --> 00:42:32.912
It write behavioral requirement,
create a safer reusable default,

00:42:33.183 --> 00:42:38.093
separate the source ownership for
the outcome ownership, give release

00:42:38.167 --> 00:42:43.374
authority enough information to act,
and reconnect high-risk WordPress change

00:42:43.379 --> 00:42:45.026
to the behavior that they can affect.

00:42:45.808 --> 00:42:48.281
So the final principle is very simple.

00:42:48.779 --> 00:42:53.116
A fut- a feature is not complete
because every internal state is passes.

00:42:53.709 --> 00:42:57.288
It is complete when the expected
behavior survive the complete

00:42:57.312 --> 00:42:59.667
WordPress system and the next chain.

00:43:00.752 --> 00:43:06.368
This is how accessibility stop being a
recurring repair project and become part

00:43:06.399 --> 00:43:08.497
of the professional WordPress engineering.

00:43:09.214 --> 00:43:12.910
The next time you install WordPress
on hinder- inherited existing

00:43:12.912 --> 00:43:17.109
site, do not look only at the
page where the problem appears.

00:43:17.504 --> 00:43:23.518
Look at the decision, component, handoff,
and update path that allow it to survive.

00:43:24.522 --> 00:43:26.292
Thank you very much for joining me.

00:43:26.486 --> 00:43:31.154
I hope this framework, uh, helped
you to make accessibility part of how

00:43:31.183 --> 00:43:35.227
your WordPress project are planned,
built, released, and maintained.

00:43:38.926 --> 00:43:39.560
All right.

00:43:40.118 --> 00:43:43.519
Thanks so much, Osdeibi, and I see
you're here in the room with us as well.

